<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to the Block member model

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Members-->
<docs:Members xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to the Block member model
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: Block member model overview and how Properties, Aggregations, Associations, and
    Capabilities relate, grounded in org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition and
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.DefinitionModel. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="def:MemberDefinition" relation="Requires">
      Abstract base for all four declared member kinds.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:DefinitionModel" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — Definition branch hierarchy including all member kinds.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Members</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Overview</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Guide</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="One member model, four kinds">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Block declares everything about its structure and behaviour through exactly
four kinds of members: Properties, Aggregations, Associations, and Capabilities. Each kind
serves a distinct purpose, and together they cover everything a Block can hold or do.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="The four member kinds">
          <content>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A Property holds a single scalar or structured value." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="An Aggregation owns a collection of child Block instances." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="An Association points to another Block instance without owning it." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A Capability defines behaviour that a Block exposes or performs." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Choosing the right kind for what you need">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>The right member kind follows from what the member needs to do — hold a single
value, own a collection of child instances, point to another instance without owning it, or
expose behaviour. Matching the need to the kind up front avoids reaching for the wrong member
shape later.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="One shared authoring pattern">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>All four member kinds are declared the same way, following one shared base
member model. A declaration either introduces a new member, or deliberately overrides one
already inherited from a base Block, and an override must say so explicitly rather than being
implied by name alone.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Deeper treatment on its own page">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Each of these four kinds gets its own following page, going deeper into how it
is declared and used.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Properties">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Properties" summary="Learn Property as a scalar or structured value member.">
                  <target>docs:Properties</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Members>
